(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-2-exam-1-ocular-exam-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Advanced Ocular History & Examination Quiz 2 — PD2 Exam 1","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"In a trauma patient, what must NOT be done?","choices":["Do not take a history","Do not palpate the globe","Do not measure visual acuity","Do not inspect the lids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is this critical in trauma. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which two tests together make up the best confrontation field examination?","choices":["Tonometry and fundoscopy","The static finger wiggle test and the kinetic red target test","The swinging light test together with the cover-uncover test","The pinhole test and the Snellen chart"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is the two. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which four features make up Horner syndrome?","choices":["A large slow pupil with reduced light reaction and a slow near reaction","Ptosis, miosis of the affected eye, anhidrosis of the ipsilateral face","Ptosis, mydriasis, lateral deviation, and a fixed pupil","Lid lag, proptosis, chemosis and diplopia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — and the preserved reaction is what separates it from a third nerve palsy. A small pupil that still reacts to light and near. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Entropion and ectropion both affect which eyelid?","choices":["Neither — they affect the conjunctiva","The lower lid","The upper lid","Both lids equally"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is the lower lid for both. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What causes SENILE ptosis?","choices":["Compression of the oculomotor nerve","Weakened muscle, relaxed tissues","Antibody blockade at the neuromuscular junction of the levator","Interruption of the sympathetic supply"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the three-part mechanical explanation. The weight of herniated fat. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Why may a zygomatic fracture cause pain on opening the mouth?","choices":["The masseter originates on the mandible and inserts on the zygoma","The infraorbital nerve is compressed during jaw movement","The temporomandibular joint is always involved","The temporalis muscle passes medial to the arch and inserts on the mandible"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the anatomical explanation Beck gave alongside the finding. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"Watery or mucoid discharge suggests which conditions?","choices":["Traumatic","Allergic or viral","Bacterial infection","Fungal infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the pairing for watery and mucoid discharge. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A temporal field defect in one eye should prompt what?","choices":["Testing for a nasal defect in the other eye","Immediate fundoscopy of the same eye only","Repeating acuity in the same eye","Measuring intraocular pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the material asks you to test that possibility explicitly. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What is interrupted in Horner syndrome?","choices":["The oculomotor nerve","The optic nerve","The sympathetic innervation of the pupil and the levator palpebrae superioris","The parasympathetic supply travelling through the ciliary ganglion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — which explains both the small pupil and the ptosis. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"After a chemical or fluid splash to the eye, what must you establish?","choices":["The manufacturer of the product","The acidity or alkalinity of the fluid","The volume of fluid involved","Whether the patient was wearing contact lenses"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is knowing this essential. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which two conditions are associated with blepharitis?","choices":["Hypothyroidism and iron deficiency","Graves disease and myasthenia gravis","Rheumatoid arthritis and lupus","Bacterial infection and atopic dermatitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the two associations to know. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is the rule about topical anaesthetic in corneal abrasion?","choices":["It should be prescribed for home use to control the pain","It should never be used at all","It is only useful if the cornea is hazy","It brings immediate relief"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the material draws exactly this line. But should be used for DIAGNOSIS and not as a treatment. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 120"},{"q":"Injection localised JUST AROUND THE CORNEA suggests which three conditions?","choices":["Keratitis, iritis, or acute glaucoma","Conjunctivitis, blepharitis, or dry eye","Subconjunctival haemorrhage, pinguecula, or pterygium","Episcleritis, chalazion, or hordeolum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the three, and this is the pattern that must not be dismissed as conjunctivitis. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What is the warning about pressure on a globe that may be ruptured?","choices":["Failing to diagnose a penetrated or ruptured globe","Pressure is required to assess the depth of injury","Pressure is harmless once a topical anaesthetic has been instilled","Pressure should be applied only with a cotton bud"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is both the missed diagnosis and the pressure. Placing too much pressure on it, is a listed mistake. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which cause of red eye shows redness that is DIFFUSE and maximal peripherally, with vision unaffected?","choices":["Glaucoma","Corneal injury or infection","Conjunctivitis","Acute iritis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — diffuse peripheral redness with preserved vision is the conjunctivitis column. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 48 (image only)"},{"q":"Why does moving the light to the affected eye cause BOTH pupils to dilate in an afferent defect?","choices":["The oculomotor nerve is being compressed on that side","The ciliary ganglion has degenerated","The afferent stimulus on that side is reduced","The affected pupil is pharmacologically blocked"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the explanation of the mechanism. So the efferent signal to both pupils is reduced and a net dilation results. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Deep eye pain is associated with which condition?","choices":["Allergic conjunctivitis","Corneal abrasion","Blepharitis","Acute narrow angle glaucoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is deep pain to this. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Why did Beck ask the class to know the referral list specifically?","choices":["Because it happens to come last in the material","Because it duplicates PD1 content","Because it appears in the textbook","Because they will be the ones making those dispositions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — her stated reason: 'you're going to be making those dispos'. — Lecture recording, 26 August 2026"},{"q":"Lateral sparseness of the eyebrows suggests what?","choices":["Hypothyroidism","Hyperthyroidism","Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Iron deficiency"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the association for lateral eyebrow loss. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Besides senile and congenital forms, which three causes of ptosis are there?","choices":["Myasthenia gravis, oculomotor nerve damage","Bell palsy, trigeminal neuralgia and stroke","Thyroid eye disease, orbital cellulitis and orbital tumour","Blepharitis, chalazion and dermatochalasis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the three, spanning neuromuscular junction, motor nerve and sympathetic chain. Damage to the sympathetic supply (Horner syndrome). — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which pair of diagnoses must be differentiated because the latter can lead to death?","choices":["Episcleritis from scleritis","Cataract from glaucoma","Hordeolum from chalazion","Preseptal from orbital cellulitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is exactly this reason. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"On the red-eye chart, what is the PUPIL like in glaucoma?","choices":["May be small and, with time, irregular","Not affected unless iritis develops","Dilated and fixed","Not affected"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the chart's glaucoma column, and a cardinal danger sign. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 48 (image only)"},{"q":"Which examination comes FIRST in the suggested order?","choices":["Direct ophthalmoscopy","Ocular pressure","Inspection","Visual acuity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the order begins with inspection. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What should you do if nystagmus is seen on lateral gaze?","choices":["Refer immediately regardless","Measure the intraocular pressure","Bring the finger back into binocular vision","Repeat the whole test with the other eye covered"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — persistence within the binocular field is what makes it abnormal. If the nystagmus persists there, consider a neurologic condition. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"When should the lacrimal sac compression test be avoided?","choices":["If the patient has previously had cataract surgery on that eye","If the patient is over sixty-five","If the area is significantly inflamed or tender","If the patient wears contact lenses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is to avoid it in that situation. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A BLUE sclera indicates what?","choices":["Long-term steroid use","Osteogenesis imperfecta","Liver disease","Thyroid eye disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the association for blue sclerae. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Eye pain with PHOTOPHOBIA suggests what?","choices":["Inflammation of the iris","Corneal abrasion","Temporal arteritis","Conjunctivitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the pairing for photophobia. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What does the cover-uncover test reveal?","choices":["A slight muscle imbalance not otherwise seen","An afferent pupillary defect of the optic nerve","A refractive error","A field defect"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the stated purpose. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"On the red-eye chart, what is the CORNEA like in glaucoma?","choices":["Steamy and cloudy","Clear","Clear or slightly clouded","Changes depending on the cause"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the chart's glaucoma column. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 48 (image only)"},{"q":"What is the correct technique for everting the upper lid to search for a foreign body?","choices":["Ask the patient to look up and relax, then pull the upper lid directly upward","Press on the globe itself to push the lid outward","Use a cotton bud placed at the lid margin itself","Ask the patient to look down and relax"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the step-by-step, including the 1 cm landmark. Raise the lid slightly so the lashes protrude, grasp the lashes and pull down and forward, then place a stick at least 1 cm above the lid margin at the upper border of the tarsal plate and push down as you raise the lid edge. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 42"}]};
